1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:05,790
This is Report Test Buddy — an agentic front-end validation tool.

2
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:10,670
We use this for performing front-end report validation.

3
00:00:11,070 --> 00:00:14,270
Basically, we perform three types of checks using this tool.

4
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:22,510
First is front-end versus front-end — meaning a production front-end screen versus a pre-production front-end screen.

5
00:00:22,830 --> 00:00:23,550
Second is

6
00:00:24,110 --> 00:00:34,750
front-end versus front-end within the same environment — meaning within production only, we are validating two different screens.

7
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:39,470
Third is front-end versus back-end validation.

8
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:45,631
So basically, we are comparing the front-end report data against back-end warehouse data.

9
00:00:45,951 --> 00:00:51,711
This is our main homepage or dashboard where we can see the run summary

10
00:00:52,991 --> 00:00:55,311
and key metrics at a glance.

11
00:00:55,631 --> 00:01:03,271
First is total scenarios — how many test cases ran, out of which how many passed and how many failed.

12
00:01:03,831 --> 00:01:09,071
We can also view the report summary filtered by report or by brand.

13
00:01:09,711 --> 00:01:14,191
Below that, you can see the detailed run summary.

14
00:01:14,991 --> 00:01:22,111
Now let me walk you through the different tabs and screens in this tool.

15
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:52,351
There is another screen called Reports. We use this for configuring and adding new reports. You can add reports using this option. Let me show you how to add a sample report. You need to click on Add Report.

16
00:01:52,911 --> 00:01:58,911
Then select Create New Report and enter the name of the report.

17
00:02:04,031 --> 00:02:11,471
In the Screen Name field, we enter the screen name — for example, a sample screen name.

18
00:02:17,432 --> 00:02:24,152
Then in the Screen URL field, we provide the exact screen URL.

19
00:02:34,432 --> 00:02:41,712
Here we need to enter the reference screen URL — in this case, the pre-production URL.

20
00:02:45,392 --> 00:02:57,072
Now for the login credentials — the first credential is for our main screen in production, and the second is the reference screen credential.

21
00:02:58,591 --> 00:03:00,192
Then the warehouse credential.

22
00:03:00,352 --> 00:03:11,472
If you want to perform a warehouse check — that is, front-end versus back-end validation — you need to enter the database connection details as well.

23
00:03:11,792 --> 00:03:22,152
And if you have any SQL templates, we will cover that going forward.

24
00:03:22,832 --> 00:03:24,712
Now I will click Save.

25
00:03:28,192 --> 00:03:35,312
Now I will go to the report screen to create a test scenario.

26
00:03:35,312 --> 00:03:45,593
Basically, we need to provide a prompt in natural language describing the test case to validate the

27
00:04:13,073 --> 00:04:13,953
reference report.

28
00:04:21,322 --> 00:04:24,043
Now we click on Generate Scenario.

29
00:04:36,093 --> 00:04:47,773
You can add a test case in two ways — either provide a full description of the case in this text box and click Generate, or enter the scenario name manually. Then we have options such as

30
00:04:49,793 --> 00:04:52,433
different types of cases: Warehouse Match, Reference Match.

31
00:04:52,753 --> 00:04:55,793
In Warehouse Match, we compare data from front-end to back-end.

32
00:04:56,033 --> 00:05:01,473
In Reference Match, we compare data front-end versus front-end.

33
00:05:01,473 --> 00:05:11,153
In Trend Check, we validate the trend of a graph. Then we have Range Check, Functional Check, and so on.

34
00:05:11,393 --> 00:05:16,594
We also have a Criticality dropdown to set the priority level —

35
00:05:16,914 --> 00:05:21,474
Low, Medium, High, or Critical. This one is set to Medium.

36
00:05:21,554 --> 00:05:25,234
I click Add, and the test case is created.

37
00:05:25,554 --> 00:05:34,874
Now inside this scenario, I need to provide a description in natural language — for example, load the Overview

38
00:06:51,395 --> 00:06:55,715
screen. This prompt drives the script generation.

39
00:06:55,955 --> 00:07:13,155
Here we configure the assertion — whether each KPI should be equal to reference, less than or equal to reference, less than reference, or any other comparison operator.

40
00:07:13,635 --> 00:07:20,195
Next, we need to add the filter combination so that our

41
00:07:21,475 --> 00:07:26,195
script knows which page filters to apply. We are configuring this

42
00:07:30,435 --> 00:07:31,395
Overview page.

43
00:07:31,555 --> 00:07:33,475
So this is our Overview page.

44
00:07:36,915 --> 00:07:38,355
This is the production Overview page.

45
00:07:38,595 --> 00:07:45,555
We are validating this KPI number against the QA page — that is, this reference number.

46
00:07:46,195 --> 00:07:46,675
So,

47
00:07:47,395 --> 00:08:00,765
we have different filters on this page, and we need to configure those filters here as well. Let me do that — Area,

48
00:08:20,916 --> 00:09:02,266
Region, Territory, and Time Bucket — that will be YTD. I save this filter combination. Now, once I have provided the prompt and the filter combination, I click Generate Script. Our back-end agent will start working and generate the script.

49
00:09:05,636 --> 00:09:31,236
It typically takes a few attempts to generate and validate the script. Now our agent has completed — this is the script generated by the agent. We also have a reference script option, and we can regenerate or use the generated main script by swapping URLs.

50
00:09:31,636 --> 00:09:43,157
We click on that. Now, to run the script, we can test it using the headless browser — it will execute the script and return the actual result.

51
00:09:45,637 --> 00:09:47,717
I will run the reference script as well.

52
00:09:49,637 --> 00:09:54,357
Now both scripts have run and we have the results.

53
00:09:55,557 --> 00:09:58,437
We ran the scripts and got the results.

54
00:09:58,997 --> 00:10:07,637
You can see that the tool scraped the KPI values from the main page, and it also scraped the reference values.

55
00:10:08,037 --> 00:10:14,917
In the Latest Result section, we can see that the actual UI values match the reference UI values.

56
00:10:15,637 --> 00:10:24,757
So this is how you set up a test case and run it manually. Now,

57
00:10:25,317 --> 00:10:33,797
moving to the next part — if you want to run an entire report suite, navigate to this screen.

58
00:10:34,277 --> 00:10:41,717
You can run the report from here, or run any screen using any of the given options.

59
00:10:42,197 --> 00:10:48,197
Now to check the test execution status, go to the Test Execution tab.

60
00:10:48,677 --> 00:10:52,357
Here you will find all the status reports and their results.

61
00:10:52,757 --> 00:10:59,717
You can filter the test cases by their results — for example, selecting Passed, you can see

62
00:11:00,197 --> 00:11:01,477
these are the passed cases.

63
00:11:01,477 --> 00:11:13,078
You can view the exact KPI values for both the actual and reference results.

64
00:11:13,638 --> 00:11:17,478
Similarly,

65
00:11:17,638 --> 00:11:20,198
you can check the failed reports.

66
00:11:20,838 --> 00:11:22,118
Why did this one fail?

67
00:11:22,118 --> 00:11:24,118
You can see the difference highlighted here.

68
00:11:24,918 --> 00:11:26,518
Next is the Runs tab.

69
00:11:26,918 --> 00:11:35,078
Here you can see the latest run or previous runs. Related to SQL Templates —

70
00:11:35,078 --> 00:11:38,238
here you can view and manage SQL templates.

71
00:11:38,918 --> 00:11:55,318
For admin-related settings, we have an Admin option under Settings, where you can

72
00:11:56,238 --> 00:12:02,758
enter the credentials for the database or your reporting login credentials.
